          },
          {
            "alt": "A photograph is shown of a parked car plugged into a charging station in a paved parking area. The parking area is situated \n            in a wooded area. People are walking in the background in the park-like atmosphere.",
            "caption": [
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "em": true,
                    "text": "Electric vehicles contain batteries that can be recharged, thereby using electric energy to bring about a chemical change. and vice versa. (Credit: Modification of work by Robert Couse-Baker.)"
                  }
                ],
                "type": "p"
              }
            ],
            "children": [
              {
                "id": "exo4lucel1l1ltg",
                "text": " ",
                "type": "text"
              }
            ],
            "id": "mbj04qbbuz2n89o",
            "src": "https://d2xvti2irp4c7t.cloudfront.net/media/f0/f02f42b0dd90d6c2045102f4e0ff9856/CNX_Chem_17_00_EVCharging.jpg",
            "type": "img",
            "width": "600"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Electrochemistry is the study of chemical reactions that produce electricity and the changes associated with the passage of electrical current through matter. All electrochemical reactions involve electron transfer, so they are "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "oxidation-reduction (or redox) reactions"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A chemical reaction in which electrons are transferred from one reactant to another. Also called redox reaction."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "518235085",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "3031256394",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": "."
              }
            ],
            "id": "p696icz54n55jf4",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Oxidation-reduction reactions provide the energy to start, and in some cases power, our cars and to run our cell phones, computers, and the numerous other battery-powered devices we rely on every day. (A battery is a type of "
              },
              {
                "children": [
                  {
                    "text": "electrochemical cell"
                  }
                ],
                "content": [
                  {
                    "children": [
                      {
                        "text": "A device that generates electrical energy from spontaneous chemical reactions (galvanic cells) or uses electrical energy to drive nonspontaneous reactions (electrolytic cells)."
                      }
                    ],
                    "id": "138769553",
                    "type": "p"
                  }
                ],
                "id": "1990829669",
                "trigger": "hover",
                "type": "popup"
              },
              {
                "text": ": a device that converts chemical energy into electrical energy.)"
              }
            ],
            "id": "u9wudo1rsisjuiv",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Electrochemical reactions are used to produce pure metals from their ores and to manufacture important industrial chemicals. A wide range of natural processes also occur via redox reactions, including photosynthesis in plants and respiration in animals. In this unit, we explore the relationships between chemical reactions and electricity, examine the principles behind the construction and operation of electrochemical cells, and consider many important applications of oxidation-reduction reactions. These reactions include both spontaneous electrochemical reactions, which produce electricity that can be converted into useful work, and nonspontaneous reactions that can be made to occur using electrical energy."
              }
            ],
            "id": "0rq4mtw240ekmqq",
            "type": "p"
          },
